# SkillsBench example dataset — illustrates the JSONL schema.
# Lines starting with '#' and blank lines are ignored by the loader.
# Each non-comment line is a JSON object with these fields:
#   id              (string)  — stable identifier; auto-generated if missing
#   query           (string)  — the prompt sent to /v1/rae/compile
#   gold_skill_ids  (uuid[])  — the truth set against which selected_skills is scored
#   gold_subunit_kinds (string[], optional) — restrict subunit recall scoring
#   task_id         (string, optional) — propagated to skills-rae for trace correlation
#   notes           (string, optional) — author commentary; ignored by the runner
#
# Authoring guidance:
#   - Replace the placeholder UUIDs below with real evo.skills.id values
#     from your corpus. Run:
#       psql -c "SELECT id, name FROM evo.skills WHERE name ILIKE '%foo%' LIMIT 5"
#     to find the gold IDs for each query.
#   - For an MVP labelled set, bootstrap with `skillsbench self-gold` and
#     then hand-curate the result so the queries don't trivially match
#     the skill name.
#
# The placeholders below WILL NOT score well against any real corpus —
# they exist only so the schema is greppable from the harness's source tree.
{"id":"example-1","query":"rename a function across a Go module","gold_skill_ids":["00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001"]}
{"id":"example-2","query":"add a database migration with rollback","gold_skill_ids":["00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002"]}
{"id":"example-3","query":"profile a Go binary with pprof","gold_skill_ids":["00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003","00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000004"]}
